      question: "Explain the threats to computer security including malware, phishing and social engineering. Describe technical and non-technical measures to protect systems. (8 marks)",
      answer: "Malware types: Viruses — attach to legitimate files and spread when executed. Worms — self-replicate across networks without a host file. Ransomware — encrypts user files and demands payment for the decryption key. Trojans — disguised as legitimate software to gain system access. Spyware — covertly monitors activity and exfiltrates data. Phishing — deceptive emails/websites impersonating legitimate organisations to steal credentials or install malware. Spear phishing targets specific individuals. Smishing (SMS) and vishing (voice) are variants. Social engineering exploits human psychology: pretexting (fabricating a scenario), baiting (leaving infected USB drives), tailgating (following staff through secure doors). Technical protections: firewalls filter network traffic; anti-malware software detects and quarantines threats; two-factor authentication prevents credential theft; encryption (AES, RSA) protects data in transit and at rest; regular patching closes known vulnerabilities; intrusion detection systems monitor for anomalous behaviour. Non-technical protections: staff security awareness training (e.g. how to identify phishing emails); strong password policies (minimum 12 characters, no reuse, use of password manager); principle of least privilege (users only access data they need); physical security (door locks, ID badges, CCTV); acceptable use policies; regular security audits and penetration testing. Evaluation: technology alone cannot protect against well-executed social engineering — human training is equally important. A layered defence-in-depth approach is most effective.",
